{"answer":"No. The calculation does not prove that sqrt(2) is rational; it produces a contradiction from rationality. With the justified choice of a lowest-denominator representation below, the contradiction proves that sqrt(2) is irrational. The original presentation was incomplete unless it supplied the reduction-to-lowest-terms justification.","approaches":[{"description":"Use reductio: assume sqrt(2) is rational, select a representation with least positive denominator, prove it has no common factor, then derive that both numerator and denominator are even.","id":"reductio-parity","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The least-denominator argument supplies the missing bridge from arbitrary rationality to a lowest-terms representation."}],"change_log":["Replaced the unjustified assumption of a lowest-terms representation with a least-positive-denominator construction.","Added explicit applications of the Well-Ordering Principle and Division Algorithm.","Scoped the temporary assumption as rationality alone and discharged it at the contradiction step.","Revised the conclusion: the argument refutes rationality rather than proves it.","No computational checks are proposed."],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Definition stated in step-rational-definition.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"rational-definition","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"A real number is rational if it equals p/q for integers p,q with q nonzero; it is irrational if it is not rational.","step_ids":["step-rational-definition"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"local_assumption","basis_reference":"Temporary negation of the desired irrationality conclusion.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"id":"assume-rational","kind":"assumption","scope_step_ids":["step-assume-rational","step-denominator-set","step-minimal-denominator","step-lowest-terms","step-square","step-a-even","step-substitute","step-b-even","step-contradiction"],"statement":"Assume temporarily that sqrt(2) is rational.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from the temporary rationality assumption by changing a nonzero denominator to its absolute value if necessary.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["assume-rational"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"denominator-set","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"There is a nonempty set S of positive integers that occur as denominators in integer representations sqrt(2)=a/b.","step_ids":["step-denominator-set"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Well-Ordering Principle: every nonempty set of positive integers has a least element; applied in step-minimal-denominator to S.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["denominator-set"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"minimal-denominator","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"There are integers a and b with b positive, sqrt(2)=a/b, and b least among the positive denominators in S.","step_ids":["step-minimal-denominator"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"A common divisor greater than 1 would produce a smaller positive denominator, contradicting minimality.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["minimal-denominator"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"lowest-terms","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The selected integers a and b have no common positive divisor greater than 1.","step_ids":["step-lowest-terms"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by squaring sqrt(2)=a/b and multiplying by the nonzero number b^2.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["minimal-denominator"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"square-equation","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"a^2=2b^2.","step_ids":["step-square"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"standard_result","basis_reference":"Division Algorithm for divisor 2: every integer n is either 2k or 2k+1 for an integer k; applied in steps step-a-even and step-b-even.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"parity-dichotomy","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Every integer is even or odd in the form 2k or 2k+1.","step_ids":["step-a-even","step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"If a were odd, its square would be odd, contrary to a^2=2b^2.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["square-equation","parity-dichotomy"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"a-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"There is an integer c such that a=2c.","step_ids":["step-a-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Substitute a=2c into a^2=2b^2 and cancel 2.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["a-even","square-equation"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-square-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"b^2=2c^2.","step_ids":["step-substitute"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"If b were odd, its square would be odd, contrary to b^2=2c^2.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["b-square-even","parity-dichotomy"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"b-even","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"b is even.","step_ids":["step-b-even"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The contradiction discharges the temporary assumption that sqrt(2) is rational.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["rational-definition","assume-rational","lowest-terms","a-even","b-even"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"sqrt2-irrational","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"sqrt(2) is irrational.","step_ids":["step-contradiction"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"step-rational-definition","justification":"This fixes the meanings of rational and irrational used in the proof.","statement":"A number is rational exactly when it is p/q for integers p,q with q nonzero; irrational means not rational."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-rational-definition"],"id":"step-assume-rational","justification":"This begins a reductio subproof by assuming the negation of irrationality.","statement":"Assume sqrt(2)=p/q for some integers p,q with q nonzero."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-assume-rational"],"id":"step-denominator-set","justification":"If q is positive, use p/q; if q is negative, use (-p)/(-q). Thus some positive integer denominator occurs.","statement":"Let S be the set of positive integers n for which sqrt(2)=m/n for some integer m; then S is nonempty."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-denominator-set"],"id":"step-minimal-denominator","justification":"By the Well-Ordering Principle, S has a least element b. By the definition of S, choose an integer a with sqrt(2)=a/b.","statement":"Choose b least in S and an integer a such that b>0 and sqrt(2)=a/b."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-minimal-denominator"],"id":"step-lowest-terms","justification":"If a and b had a common positive divisor d>1, write a=da' and b=db' with integers a',b'. Then b'=b/d is a positive integer smaller than b and sqrt(2)=a'/b', so b' is in S, contradicting the minimality of b.","statement":"a and b have no common positive divisor greater than 1."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-minimal-denominator"],"id":"step-square","justification":"Squaring sqrt(2)=a/b gives 2=a^2/b^2. Since b is positive and hence nonzero, multiplication by b^2 gives a^2=2b^2.","statement":"a^2=2b^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square"],"id":"step-a-even","justification":"The equation makes a^2 even. By the Division Algorithm for divisor 2, a is 2c or 2c+1. In the latter case, a^2=4c^2+4c+1 is odd; hence a=2c for some integer c.","statement":"There is an integer c such that a=2c."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-square","step-a-even"],"id":"step-substitute","justification":"Substitution gives 4c^2=2b^2; cancelling the nonzero integer 2 gives b^2=2c^2.","statement":"b^2=2c^2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-substitute"],"id":"step-b-even","justification":"The equation makes b^2 even. By the Division Algorithm for divisor 2, an odd b would have an odd square, so b is even.","statement":"b is even."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["step-lowest-terms","step-a-even","step-b-even"],"id":"step-contradiction","justification":"Steps step-a-even and step-b-even show that 2 divides both a and b, contradicting step-lowest-terms. This discharges the temporary assumption in step-assume-rational.","statement":"The temporary assumption that sqrt(2) is rational yields a contradiction; therefore sqrt(2) is irrational."}],"question_status":"refuted","tool_requests":[]}
